1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,993
Bangude in Kannada.

2
00:00:01,993 --> 00:00:03,479
Ayla in Malayalam.

3
00:00:03,479 --> 00:00:04,966
Bangda in Marathi.

4
00:00:05,366 --> 00:00:06,806
Three different names.

5
00:00:06,806 --> 00:00:08,800
But it is the very same fish.

6
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:13,064
Diya heard all three names at
one fish stall in Bengaluru.

7
00:00:13,064 --> 00:00:17,858
She asked: if every language has its
own name, which name does a scientist

8
00:00:17,858 --> 00:00:18,758
use?

9
00:00:18,719 --> 00:00:22,963
A scientist in any country calls
this fish Rastrelliger kanagurta.

10
00:00:22,963 --> 00:00:28,178
Today you learn how that name is built,
and the rules every name must follow.

11
00:00:28,828 --> 00:00:30,777
First, why local names fail.

12
00:00:31,127 --> 00:00:33,165
A local name is a common name.

13
00:00:33,165 --> 00:00:37,325
It changes from one language to
the next, and from place to place.

14
00:00:37,775 --> 00:00:42,223
So a scientist in Japan and a scientist
in Kerala could talk about the

15
00:00:42,223 --> 00:00:44,260
same fish and never know it.

16
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:49,359
Biology fixes this with one scientific
name for each organism, used all

17
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:50,600
over the world.

18
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:53,671
Giving names this way
is called nomenclature.

19
00:00:54,321 --> 00:00:56,764
Before naming, there is one step.

20
00:00:56,764 --> 00:01:00,169
We must first know exactly
which organism we have.

21
00:01:00,169 --> 00:01:01,805
That is identification.

22
00:01:02,155 --> 00:01:03,350
Then the name.

23
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:08,025
The system was given by Carolus
Linnaeus, and every scientific name has

24
00:01:08,025 --> 00:01:09,273
just two words.

25
00:01:09,723 --> 00:01:12,806
That is why it is called
binomial nomenclature.

26
00:01:12,806 --> 00:01:15,428
Bi means two, and nomial means names.

27
00:01:16,078 --> 00:01:18,798
Let's take one name apart, piece by piece.

28
00:01:19,148 --> 00:01:21,311
Mangifera indica, the mango.

29
00:01:21,811 --> 00:01:24,790
The first word, Mangifera,
is the generic name.

30
00:01:24,790 --> 00:01:26,334
It tells you the genus.

31
00:01:26,834 --> 00:01:30,063
The second word, indica,
is the specific epithet.

32
00:01:30,063 --> 00:01:32,814
It picks out one species
inside that genus.

33
00:01:33,314 --> 00:01:35,285
At the end, you may see Linn.

34
00:01:35,285 --> 00:01:37,743
That is the author's name, written short.

35
00:01:37,743 --> 00:01:40,423
It means Linnaeus first
described this species.

36
00:01:40,923 --> 00:01:44,627
And both words are printed in
italics, the sloping letters.

37
00:01:44,627 --> 00:01:46,242
The author name is not.

38
00:01:46,892 --> 00:01:49,659
Now run any name through
the four rule gates.

39
00:01:50,009 --> 00:01:50,909
Gate one.

40
00:01:50,851 --> 00:01:53,716
Is it in Latin, or made to look Latin?

41
00:01:53,716 --> 00:01:58,553
Biological names are Latin or Latinised,
whatever language they came from.

42
00:01:59,303 --> 00:02:00,203
Gate two.

43
00:02:00,042 --> 00:02:03,688
Are there two parts, genus
first, then the specific epithet?

44
00:02:04,438 --> 00:02:05,338
Gate three.

45
00:02:05,253 --> 00:02:09,320
Does the genus start with a capital
letter, and the epithet with a small

46
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:10,220
letter?

47
00:02:10,654 --> 00:02:11,554
Gate four.

48
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,310
Is it printed in italics?

49
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:17,389
When you write by hand, underline
each word separately instead.

50
00:02:18,139 --> 00:02:19,039
Watch.

51
00:02:18,788 --> 00:02:22,597
Solanum tuberosum, the
potato, passes all four gates.

52
00:02:22,597 --> 00:02:27,450
But mangifera Indica, with the
capitals swapped, stops at gate three.

53
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:29,450
Who makes these rules?

54
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:34,604
For plants, the International Code for
Botanical Nomenclature, called ICBN.

55
00:02:35,054 --> 00:02:39,686
For animals, the International Code
of Zoological Nomenclature, called

56
00:02:39,686 --> 00:02:40,586
ICZN.

57
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:42,396
Same idea, two rule books.

58
00:02:43,046 --> 00:02:45,186
Now compare the two kinds of names.

59
00:02:45,536 --> 00:02:46,617
A common name.

60
00:02:46,617 --> 00:02:48,272
Bangude, ayla, bangda.

61
00:02:48,272 --> 00:02:51,917
Many names for one fish, and
they change with the place.

62
00:02:52,367 --> 00:02:53,719
A scientific name.

63
00:02:53,719 --> 00:02:55,263
Rastrelliger kanagurta.

64
00:02:55,263 --> 00:02:58,922
One name, the same in every
country, in every book.

65
00:02:59,372 --> 00:03:00,272
Your turn.

66
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:03,216
A notebook says Panthera
Leo, for the lion.

67
00:03:03,216 --> 00:03:04,904
Is it written correctly?

68
00:03:04,904 --> 00:03:06,182
Pause and decide.

69
00:03:09,382 --> 00:03:10,282
No!

70
00:03:09,833 --> 00:03:12,660
The epithet must start
with a small letter.

71
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:15,683
The correct name is
Panthera leo, in italics.

72
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:17,443
Remember this.

73
00:03:17,443 --> 00:03:19,570
Genus first, with a capital.

74
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:21,530
Epithet second, all small.

75
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:22,806
Both in italics.

76
00:03:23,406 --> 00:03:24,999
Now, a worked example.

77
00:03:24,999 --> 00:03:27,714
Let's fix three names
from a student's notes.

78
00:03:28,064 --> 00:03:28,964
One.

79
00:03:28,665 --> 00:03:30,161
solanum tuberosum.

80
00:03:30,161 --> 00:03:32,529
The genus needs a capital S.

81
00:03:32,529 --> 00:03:34,025
Solanum tuberosum.

82
00:03:34,475 --> 00:03:35,375
Two.

83
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:36,401
Homo Sapiens.

84
00:03:36,401 --> 00:03:38,930
The epithet needs a small s.

85
00:03:38,930 --> 00:03:40,214
Homo sapiens.

86
00:03:40,664 --> 00:03:41,564
Three.

87
00:03:41,282 --> 00:03:45,376
Musca domestica, handwritten,
with one line under both words.

88
00:03:45,376 --> 00:03:47,464
Each word needs its own line.

89
00:03:47,914 --> 00:03:52,621
Now all three follow the rules, and
anyone in the world will know exactly

90
00:03:52,621 --> 00:03:54,336
which organism you mean.

91
00:03:54,986 --> 00:03:56,451
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:03:56,801 --> 00:04:00,457
Many students say the species
name of the mango is indica.

93
00:04:00,857 --> 00:04:03,513
But indica is only the specific epithet.

94
00:04:03,513 --> 00:04:07,751
The name of the species is both
words together: Mangifera indica.

95
00:04:08,401 --> 00:04:09,787
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:09,787 --> 00:04:13,946
NEET has asked for the correctly
written scientific name of the mango.

97
00:04:13,946 --> 00:04:17,565
Expect pick the correct spelling,
and rule based statements.

98
00:04:18,165 --> 00:04:19,383
Exam level now.

99
00:04:19,383 --> 00:04:21,180
Read the four statements.

100
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:22,873
Which ones are correct?

101
00:04:22,873 --> 00:04:24,142
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:29,542 --> 00:04:30,862
The answer is B.

103
00:04:30,862 --> 00:04:32,641
Statements one and three.

104
00:04:33,141 --> 00:04:34,140
Two is wrong.

105
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:37,036
The specific epithet
starts with a small letter.

106
00:04:37,536 --> 00:04:38,611
Four is wrong.

107
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:42,522
The author's name comes at the
end, after the specific epithet.

108
00:04:43,122 --> 00:04:44,584
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:44,584 --> 00:04:46,087
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:49,287 --> 00:04:52,285
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:52,685 --> 00:04:53,585
One.

112
00:04:53,175 --> 00:04:56,047
What are the two parts
of a scientific name?

113
00:04:56,947 --> 00:04:57,847
Two.

114
00:04:57,416 --> 00:05:01,123
How do you show a scientific
name when you write it by hand?

115
00:05:02,023 --> 00:05:02,923
Three.

116
00:05:02,603 --> 00:05:06,134
What does Linn mean at the
end of Mangifera indica Linn?

117
00:05:07,234 --> 00:05:11,125
Next time: a pressed leaf,
dried flat inside an old book.

118
00:05:11,125 --> 00:05:13,797
Why do scientists keep thousands like it?

119
00:05:13,797 --> 00:05:15,870
We find out in taxonomic aids.
